import { BytesInspectionCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> BytesInspectionCopy = BytesInspectionCopy {
  summary: "slice extracts a range that includes its start and excludes its end. If the range is invalid, it returns BytesSliceError in Left rather than adjusting the bounds.",
  taskTitle: "Validate a range and extract part of a byte sequence",
  purpose: "Extract positions 1 up to but not including 3 from four bytes. Allow an empty range too, while rejecting a reversed range, a negative start, and an end past the input length with an explanation.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript’s Uint8Array.slice clamps out-of-range bounds and counts negative positions from the end. It does not implement this rejection policy by itself. extract first checks integer bounds and 0 <= start <= end <= content.length, then passes only a valid range to native slice.",
  benefit: "Seseragi’s slice includes range validation in the API. Separate Either<BytesSliceError, Bytes> to handle either the extracted sequence or a failure that preserves the requested bounds.",
  setup: "import * as bytes makes std/bytes available as bytes. fn extract start: Int -> end: Int -> content: bytes.Bytes -> String takes a start, an end, and Bytes, then returns a String. bytes.slice start end content passes the three arguments in order after spaces.",
  after: [
    "The bytes.fromInts [10, 20, 30, 40] in main validates an integer array and makes Bytes. A Left error from that Either is an input failure, displayed with show. The Bytes received by Right content is the input for the range operations.",
    "The match in extract separates the Either returned by slice. Left (bytes.InvalidByteRange { start, end, length }) binds the three fields from the range-error record to local variables with the same names. start and end are the requested bounds; length is the input’s byte count.",
    "The ${start}, ${end}, and ${length} in the backtick string explain the rejected request. Right part receives successful Bytes as part, then bytes.toInts and show make a string displaying its integer array. The expression after each branch’s -> is its result.",
    "The do { ... } in Right content runs the calls and prints their results from top to bottom. extract 1 3 content produces [20, 30] from positions 1 and 2; extract 2 2 content produces an empty [].",
    "extract 3 1 content, extract (-1) 2 content, and extract 0 5 content fail. Notice the parentheses around the negative argument (-1) in the call. Printing the original content last still shows [10, 20, 30, 40].",
  ],
  details: [
    "A valid range satisfies 0 <= start <= end <= length. Because start is included and end is excluded, slice 0 length content extracts the entire sequence. start and end are Int values; fractional positions are not accepted.",
    "Equal start and end positions give an empty range. Setting both to length succeeds, and slicing empty input from 0 to 0 succeeds too. Neither bound may be negative or greater than length, and a reversed range fails.",
    "The input Bytes keeps its contents. A failure reports the original start and end with the actual length, so there is no adjusted range to infer. To use successful Bytes, receive the Right value rather than passing on the entire Either.",
  ],
}
